SWEET boot line 2048 (patch 0320, user 2026-11-12 "there has to be a better way"): arm64 BOOT_LINE_LENGTH 1024 -> 2048 on top of patch 0176, so struct boot_args grows from 1152 to 2176 bytes. Why: with --mif-clk + --pmic-src-clear the shim appends up to 165 B of sweetmif*/sweetps* tokens into the same field, which left 843 B for the host line and forced feature trims (ALL-ON profile all_on5_nav_psclr_mif). Same mechanism and the same producer/consumer argument as 0176 (research 108): the shim is the only producer, built with the same constant (shim/payload.h); readers use the macro or offsetof (genassym BA_BOOT_FLAGS); the sysctl handler's stack copy (bsd/kern/kern_sysctl.c `char buf[BOOT_LINE_LENGTH]`) becomes 2 KiB on a kernel stack of at least 16 KiB (osfmk/mach/arm/vm_param.h KERNEL_STACK_SIZE); stackshot copies MIN(strlen+1, BOOT_LINE_LENGTH) as a variable-length kcdata record; IONVRAM's check is unused (no NVRAM boot-args on this port). The 0176 tag string follows the macro, so tools/mkpayload.py refuses a 1024 kernel on a 2048 shim and vice versa. No device register, no store.

diff --git a/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/boot.h b/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/boot.h
--- a/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/boot.h
+++ b/pexpert/pexpert/arm64/boot.h
@@ -30,7 +30,11 @@
  * limit above does not bind us. Readers use the macro or the struct fields (start.s reads bootFlags
  * through genassym BA_BOOT_FLAGS = offsetof), so they move with it; sizeof(struct boot_args) 736 -> 1152.
  */
-#define BOOT_LINE_LENGTH        1024
+/*
+ * PixOS (patch 0320): 2048 on top of 0176, same reasoning; sizeof(struct boot_args) 1152 -> 2176. The shim's appended
+ * sweetmif and sweetps tokens (up to 165 B) no longer cut into the host line (ALL-ON T-02/T-24, 2026-11-12).
+ */
+#define BOOT_LINE_LENGTH        2048
 
